memory management
*Memory management is the process of allocating (giving) and deallocating
 (freeing) memory while a Java program is running.
 *Java does this automatically using the JVM (Java Virtual Machine) and
  the Garbage Collector (GC).

                  JVM Memory
        +-----------------------+
        |       Heap            |  <-- Objects, Arrays
        +-----------------------+
        |       Stack           |  <-- Method calls, Local variables
        +-----------------------+
        | Method Area (Meta)    |  <-- Class      information, Static variables
        +-----------------------+
        | PC Register           |
        +-----------------------+
        | Native Method Stack   |
        +-----------------------+

1. Stack Memory
Stores local variables.
Stores method calls.
Each method gets its own stack frame.
Memory is automatically removed when the method finishes.
2. Heap Memory
Stores objects and arrays.
Shared by all threads.
Managed by the Garbage Collector.
the memory allowgation is not continues
TWO POINTERS
1)converging pointers
 converging pointers are used to compare the char of the string 
    abcdcba
    ^     ^
    abcdcba
     ^   ^
A converging pointer is a technique where two pointers start from opposite ends of an array or string and move toward each other until they meet.

code:


public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] arr = {1,2,3,4,5,6};

        int target = 9;

        int left = 0;
        int right = arr.length - 1;

        while(left < right){

            int sum = arr[left] + arr[right];

            if(sum == target){
                System.out.println(arr[left] + " " + arr[right]);
                break;
            }
            else if(sum < target){
                left++;
            }
            else{
                right--;
            }
        }
    }
}


2)parallel pointers
In the parallel pointer technique, both pointers start from the same side (usually the left) and move in the same direction.
It is commonly used for:

Removing duplicates
Sliding window problems
Partitioning arrays
Fast and slow pointer problems

slow
 ↓
fast
 ↓
[2, 7, 11, 15, 20]

slow fast
 ↓   ↓
[1,1,2,2,3,4,4]

code:


public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] arr = {1,1,2,2,3,4,4};

        int slow = 0;

        for (int fast = 1; fast < arr.length; fast++) {

            if (arr[slow] != arr[fast]) {
                slow++;
                arr[slow] = arr[fast];
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Unique elements:");

        for (int i = 0; i <= slow; i++) {
            System.out.print(arr[i] + " ");
        }
    }
}


3)Trigger based pointers
A trigger-based pointer is a variation of the two-pointer technique where one pointer moves normally, and the second pointer moves only when a specific condition (trigger) is satisfied.

This technique is commonly used in:

Partitioning arrays
Moving zeros
Removing duplicates
Rearranging elements
[1,2,3,4,5,1]
our goal is to find a duplication numbers so 
[1,2,3,4,5,1]
 ^ ^      
[1,2,3,4,5,1]
 ^   ^
[1,2,3,4,5,1]
 ^     ^       
[1,2,3,4,5,1]
 ^       ^  
[1,2,3,4,5,1]
 ^         ^  
code:


public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] arr = {0, 1, 0, 3, 12};

        int j = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {

            if (arr[i] != 0) {   // Trigger condition
                int temp = arr[i];
                arr[i] = arr[j];
                arr[j] = temp;
                j++;
            }
        }

        for (int num : arr) {
            System.out.print(num + " ");
        }
    }
}